WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.139
We hope you've got health insurance, because
we're about to blow your minds.

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:08.970
Those who have seen 2017's Spider-Man: Homecoming
and its 2019 sequel Spider-Man: Far From Home

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.300
will remember Roger Harrington, the slightly
dorky, more-than-slightly bumbling, well-meaning

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.780
science teacher played by Martin Starr.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.340
In Homecoming, Mr. Harrington instructs the
students of Midtown School of Science and

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.210
Technology and oversees the school's Academic
Decathlon Team, of which Tom Holland's Peter

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.240
Parker is a member.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.539
And in Far From Home, the teacher tries his
hardest to make his students' summer trip

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:32.860
across Europe as wonderful as possible, but
proves hilariously incompetent.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.900
To be fair, it's not entirely his fault.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.050
Mr. Harrington is a memorable MCU character,
but Homecoming and Far From Home aren't the

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.710
only Marvel movies he's been in.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.440
As several sharp-eyed fans have pointed out,
Mr. Harrington made his MCU debut way back

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.739
in 2008, when he cameoed in The Incredible
Hulk - which, despite what your one friend

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:52.830
might insist, is indeed part of the MCU.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.700
Remember the student who smiles and waves
a slice of pizza at Edward Norton's Bruce

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.239
Banner in the chemistry lab at Culver University?

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.450
Yeah, that's Roger Harrington, credited in
the film simply as "Computer Nerd."

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:08.279
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige confirmed
the fact during an AMA session on Reddit.

00:01:08.279 --> 00:01:09.279
One fan wrote,

00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:13.130
"Martin Starr was both in the Incredible Hulk
(very briefly in the computer lab) and in

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.259
Spider-Man: Homecoming as Peter Parker's debate
coach.

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:18.229
Can we assume these are the same people?"

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:21.689
Feige replied with a simple observation, saying,
quote, "I do."

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:25.319
It makes sense that the book-smart student
became a not-so-street-smart teacher - and

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:26.770
a science teacher, at that.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.570
It also makes sense that a character from
an old film was retconned into also being

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.249
a character in a newer film, considering Marvel
also did that very thing with Peter Parker

00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:38.080
and the young man dressed as Iron Man at the
end of Iron Man 2.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.520
"Nice work, kid."

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.400
So Feige's confirmation makes this a nice
bit of Marvel trivia to share at your next

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:47.409
dinner party.

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:50.590
However, the admission that Starr's characters
from The Incredible Hulk and the Spider-Man

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.259
movies are the same person arguably puts a
tiny wrinkle in the overall Marvel canon,

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:58.669
particularly regarding a tie-in novel that
accompanied the release of The Incredible

00:01:58.669 --> 00:01:59.669
Hulk.

00:01:59.669 --> 00:02:03.290
As many fans have noted, the Incredible Hulk
novelization takes a different approach to

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:08.069
the character Starr played in the film, identifying
him as being a Korean-American genius named

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:12.519
Amadeus Cho, who was first introduced in Marvel
Comics in 2005.

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:16.640
Since Starr is definitely not Korean-American,
like Cho is, the two characters are more like

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.709
analogues to one another than anything else
- but we digress.

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:23.439
In the novel version of the story, Banner
meets Cho when he sneaks into the computer

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:26.430
lab, finding the student studying for his
final exams.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.099
Much like movie Banner does with Martin Starr's
character, Banner offers Cho some pizza, and

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:32.099
that's the end of that.

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:35.849
A scene like this was actually supposed to
be included in the movie as an extended version

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:38.030
of Starr's actual scene in the final film.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.900
The deleted scene sees Banner trying to trick
his way into the lab with the promise of free

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.209
pizza, with the excuse that someone in the
radiation lab called for it and then bailed.

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:50.439
The tactic works like a charm, getting Banner
easy access to use the computer and search

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:51.969
for the data he needs.

00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:55.000
"You, my friend, are a hero.”

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.320
Interestingly enough, the Cho character referenced
in the novelization actually ended up with

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.590
a meaty role in Marvel Comics years later,
going on to succeed Banner as the Hulk as

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.200
the star of the 2015 comic series The Totally
Awesome Hulk.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.390
The connection between Martin Starr's Incredible
Hulk character and the book's Amadeus Cho

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.709
is pretty undeniable.

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:16.209
But since Feige has since stated that the
computer nerd in The Incredible Hulk movie

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:19.849
is actually Roger Harrington, that means he's
not Amadeus Cho.

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:23.680
So in case you were wondering, that means
that the Incredible Hulk novelization officially

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.019
isn't canonical.

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:29.349
This isn't to say that Marvel fans won't ever
see Cho in the MCU one day, becoming the Jade

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:31.780
Genius after Banner somehow passes the torch.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.030
He just probably won't be played by Martin
Starr.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.049
"As you know, we made it out alive and that's
the important thing.

00:03:38.049 --> 00:03:42.080
Couldn't bear to lose a student on a school
trip.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.880
Not again."

